int-Michel across the bay.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/avungara"><span class="enc-name">Avungara</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Avungara are the ruling aristocratic clan of the Zande people of north-central Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/avvakum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/avvakum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Avvakum</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Avvakum Petrov was a Russian archpriest and leader of the Old Believers who opposed the liturgical reforms of Patriarch Nikon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awa-odori"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/awa-odori.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awa Odori</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">Awa Odori is a summer dance festival held in Tokushima, on the Japanese island of Shikoku, during the Obon season in August.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awa-province-tokushima"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/awa-province-tokushima.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awa Province (Tokushima)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Awa was an old province of Japan in the east of the island of Shikoku, corresponding to present-day Tokushima Prefecture.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awadh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/awadh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awadh</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Awadh, or Oudh, is a region of north-central Uttar Pradesh in India, between the Ganges and the Himalayan foothills.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awaji-island"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/awaji-island.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awaji Island</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Awaji Island is the largest island of Japan's Inland Sea, lying between Honshu and Shikoku and now part of Hyogo Prefecture.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awami-league"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/awami-league.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awami League</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Awami League is a political party of Bangladesh, founded in Dhaka in 1949 as a rival to the Muslim League in East Pakistan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awariku"><span class="enc-name">Awariku</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Awariku, called Urikki in Assyrian texts, was a king of Que (Hiyawa) on the Cilician plain in the 8th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awash-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/awash-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awash River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Awash River is a river in Ethiopia that flows from the central highlands towards the Afar lowlands.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awdaghast"><span class="enc-name">Awdaghast</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Awdaghast was a trading town in the southern Sahara, in present-day Mauritania, that served as a terminus of trans-Saharan caravan routes.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-awhad-ayyub"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-awhad-ayyub.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al-Awhad Ayyub</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Awhad Ayyub was an Ayyubid prince, son of al-Adil, who governed Mayyafariqin in Upper Mesopotamia for his father from about 1200.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awilum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/awilum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awīlum</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">In the Code of Hammurabi, the Babylonian legal text composed about 1753 BCE, the awīlum was one of three main kinds of person, beside the muškēnum and the slave.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awjila"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/awjila.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awjila</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Awjila is an oasis town in the Libyan Desert of eastern Libya, some 250 km inland from the Gulf of Sirte.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awlad-al-balad"><span class="enc-name">Awlad al-balad</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The awlad al-balad, meaning 'sons of the country', were the noble ruling elite of the Wadai Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awlad-al-nas"><span class="enc-name">Awlad al-nas</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Awlad al-nas, &quot;sons of respectable people&quot;, was the name for the sons of mamluks in the Mamluk Sultanate, which ruled Egypt and Syria from 1250 to 1517.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awoamefia"><span class="enc-name">Awoamefia</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Awoamefia is the title of the paramount ruler of the Anlo Ewe of south-eastern Ghana, who resides at Anloga.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awole"><span class="enc-name">Awole</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Awole was an Alaafin of the Oyo Empire in the late eighteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awori"><span class="enc-name">Awori</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Awori are a Yoruba subgroup of the coastal lagoons of south-western Nigeria, in present-day Lagos and Ogun states.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awraba"><span class="enc-name">Awraba</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Awraba were a Berber tribal confederation of north-western Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awujale"><span class="enc-name">Awujale</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Awujale is the title of the paramount ruler of the Ijebu Kingdom, a Yoruba state in south-western Nigeria with its seat at Ijebu-Ode.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awwam-temple"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/awwam-temple.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awwam Temple</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Awwam Temple, known in Arabic as Mahram Bilqis, is the great sanctuary of the god Almaqah near Marib in Yemen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/axayacatl"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/axayacatl.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Axayacatl</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Axayacatl was the sixth tlatoani of Tenochtitlan, ruling in the later fifteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/axel-oxenstierna"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/axel-oxenstierna.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Axel Oxenstierna</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Axel Oxenstierna was Lord High Chancellor of Sweden from 1612 until his death in 1654.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/axim"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/axim.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Axim</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Axim is a coastal town in the Western Region of Ghana.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/axis-powers.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Axis powers</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Axis powers were the alliance of Germany, Italy and Japan, with other partners, that fought the Allies in the Second World War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ayacucho.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ayacucho</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayacucho is a city in the central highlands of southern Peru and the capital of the region of the same name.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayanis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ayanis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ayanis</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayanis is the site of a Urartian fortress on the eastern shore of Lake Van in eastern Turkey, founded by Rusa II in the seventh century BCE and known in antiquity as…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayarmaca"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ayarmaca.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ayarmaca</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ayarmaca were an Andean people whose lordship, centred north-west of Cusco in Peru, was one of the main rivals of the early Inca kingdom.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayas-cilicia"><span class="enc-name">Ayas (Cilicia)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayas, the Laiazzo of Italian merchants, is a port on the Gulf of İskenderun in southern Turkey, now the town of Yumurtalık.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayawaso"><span class="enc-name">Ayawaso</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayawaso was the inland capital of the Ga kingdom on the Accra plains of present-day Ghana.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ayaz-kala"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ayaz-kala.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ayaz-Kala</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ayaz-Kala is a group of fortresses on and around a hill at the edge of the Kyzylkum Desert in Karakalpakstan, north-western Uzbekistan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aybak"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aybak.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aybak</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aybak, or Izz al-Din Aybak, was a Mamluk commander who became the first sultan of the Mamluk Sultanate in Egypt in 1250.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aydinoglu-mehmed-bey"><span class="enc-name">Aydınoğlu Mehmed Bey</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aydınoğlu Mehmed Bey was a commander in the service of the Germiyanids who founded the Aydinid beylik in